<section class="panel" aria-label="Grade 3 2021 Round and Round Again"><h2>Warm-up — More Groups, Fewer Groups</h2><p>5×7=35; 4×7=28; 6×7=42; 4×8=32. Explain the connections.</p><h2>Activity 1 — All the Numbers</h2><p>Use the halfway-up rounding rule for whole numbers. Record all integers for each target; your teacher checks the complete list.</p><table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Original target</th><th>First</th><th>Last</th><th>Count</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>50 to nearest 10</td><td>45</td><td>54</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>70 to nearest 10</td><td>65</td><td>74</td><td>10</td></tr><tr><td>600 to nearest 100</td><td>550</td><td>649</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>100 to nearest 100</td><td>50</td><td>149</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>500 to nearest 100</td><td>450</td><td>549</td><td>100</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The source has a 30–60 number-line image; these intervals are independently computed, not an image reproduction.</p><h2>Activity 2 — What's My Mystery Number?</h2><p>Write a secret number from100 to1,000. Supply three clues: odd/even, rounds to a named benchmark, and lies between two boundaries. Partner guesses and verbal explanations need teacher review.</p><p>First-party example: even, rounds to750 to nearest ten, strictly between745 and747. Answer:746. This does not copy the publisher's example or restricted Cool-down.</p><p><a href="https://im.kendallhunt.com/k5/teachers/grade-3/unit-3/lesson-16/lesson.html">Original 2021 teacher reference</a></p></section>